generate 

openssl req -nodes -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout example.key -out example.csr -subj "/C=GB/ST=London/L=London/O=Acme Widgets/OU=IT Department/CN=127.0.0.1" -addext "subjectAltName=IP:127.0.0.1"

openssl x509 -req -days 365 -in example.csr -signkey example.key -out example.crt -extfile <(echo "subjectAltName=IP:127.0.0.1")


check
openssl x509 -in example.crt -noout -text
find 
X509v3 extensions:
    X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:
        IP Address:127.0.0.1


explain
-addext "subjectAltName=IP:127.0.0.1": Adds the SAN for the IP address 127.0.0.1.
-extfile <(echo "subjectAltName=IP:127.0.0.1"): Ensures the SAN is included in the signed certificate.
modern browsers (since 2017) ignore CN and require a SAN that matches the accessed hostname or IP. Without a SAN, Chrome will reject the certificate.

on mac os
Launcher -> Search Keychain Access
choose 'system' on the left bar, File -> import item -> example.crt on top bar, double click this item, 
click Trust to unfold, choose SSL -> Trust Forever, then close, it will pop up auth window, input password, done

